TU1NZ / WANT ME SEEN S11.2-R15.6 S10 HEALTH CHILD-CODE DIAGNOSIS

Classification: GENERIC_S10_EXCEPTION_ENVELOPE_DROPPED_CHILD_CONTEXT
Scope: source-only; no server, runtime, evidence or product-state mutation

Observed evidence

- The R15.5 Phase-A S10 health run started at 2026-09-26T06:25:58Z and
  ended with exit status 2 and only `S10_1_WMS_HEALTH_RED`.
- The next natural timer run at 2026-09-26T06:31:01Z ended with status 0.
- Public WMS remained HTTP 200 and the S10 WMS service remained active with
  zero restarts. No R15.5 sudo, deployment, S11 start or Canary occurred.
- The failed run took approximately 33 seconds. This is consistent with a
  child process reaching the entrypoint's 30-second timeout, but the original
  generic report does not prove which child timed out. R15.6 does not invent
  that missing historical fact.

Exact loss location

The S10.1 entrypoint invokes local/public WMS, aggregate state, changeset,
growth, Community and systemd checks. Community failures already have a strict
outer/child/component contract. Every other `OSError`, `SubprocessError` and
non-canonical `ValueError` reached the final generic exception handler in
`tu1nz_adult_public_s10_1_health.py`. That handler retained a string only when
it began with `S10_`; otherwise it emitted `S10_1_WMS_HEALTH_RED`. A
`subprocess.TimeoutExpired` therefore lost both its child type and the calling
component and retained only exit class 2. The old R15.5 Phase-A check consumed
the resulting unit status and had no structured child to recover from the
journal. There is no shell-pipe, stderr, systemd or R15.4 access-contract loss
before this point.

Call graph and contracts

| Layer | Input | Output | Failure contract |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| S10 timer/service | scheduled unit | one health process | systemd result and process exit |
| S10.1 entrypoint | versioned contracts and read-only states | one bounded JSON report | general S10 or existing Community envelope |
| growth/S9 | S9 and S10 contracts | growth and channel health | existing S10 growth/Telegram child codes |
| Community/S8 | poller, lease, event path, provider, latency | Community health JSON | existing strict Community outer/child contract |
| WMS | local/public SFW endpoints | bounded health result | public/product-boundary child codes |
| state checks | aggregate, changeset, database-facing child checks | bounded state result | state/release child codes |
| systemd checks | allowlisted services/timers | active/restart/liveness result | system-state child codes |
| S10 wrapper | child exception plus bounded component | outer, child, component, classification, action and exit | missing/unknown fail closed |
| future R15.5 preflight | versioned structured JSON runs | current state plus retained last failure | STOP on current RED; never retries |

R15.6 contract

- The general outer code remains `S10_1_WMS_HEALTH_RED`.
- Existing specific S10 and Community child codes are preserved.
- General missing and unknown children map to dedicated hard-stop codes.
- A child timeout and `S10_TELEGRAM_HEALTH_RED` are diagnostic transient
  candidates only. Their next action is a read-only recheck before a new,
  separate deployment authorisation.
- Two consecutive identical transient RED observations are classified as a
  persistent blocker. Hard DB/state/release/config/product-boundary failures
  never degrade to transient.
- Each health report carries the fixed contract version and an UTC
  `observed_at` run boundary. The future parser reads only those structured
  JSON reports and ignores unstructured journal text.
- A current GREEN is represented independently from the retained most recent
  failure and recovery time. A current RED always stops Phase A.
- The A-L simulator never starts deployment and requires a separate future
  authorisation even when a natural later GREEN confirms transience.

Unchanged boundaries

The R15.4 installed-copy/runtime-access contract, Tailscale SSH, `chatops`,
sudoers, firewall, ACLs, systemd units, server runtime, databases, evidence,
Telegram state, S11 flags and Adult product boundaries are unchanged.
